        "text": "Note that each block hash is unique because the odds of finding two pieces of data that produce the exact same hash are virtually impossible. In cryptography, we call this a hash collision. It describes the situation in which two different inputs produce the same hash as output. Therefore, being resistant to hash collisions is what prevents blockchain data from being tampered with. Why is a tamper proof database like the blockchain useful? In a regular database, modifying or copying data is a straightforward process. However, that presents a problem. How do we store important data in a way that everyone can rely on? So far, the solution has been to trust the entities that store the data. Still, that trust can be misplaced and abused.",
        "start": 160,
        "end": 42460,
        "confidence": 0.98844856,
